---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PropagationChecker from '../components/PropagationChecker.astro';

const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "How long does DNS propagation take?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "It is governed by the record's TTL: each resolver may serve the old value until its cached copy expires, so worst case equals the full TTL. New names with no cached history resolve immediately everywhere." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Why do some resolvers still show old values?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Their cached copy has not expired yet. The TTL column in the results shows how much longer each resolver may keep serving the old answer." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can I speed up propagation?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Not for a change already made. For future changes, lower the TTL to 300 seconds a day before, make the change, then raise it back." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Propagation finished but I still see the old site. Why?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "The remaining cache is usually local: your browser, operating system, or ISP resolver keeps its own copy. Flush the local DNS cache and restart the browser." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Which resolvers does this checker query?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Cloudflare (1.1.1.1), Google (8.8.8.8), and AliDNS (223.5.5.5) over DNS-over-HTTPS, directly from your browser. These are three vantage points, not every network on earth." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Is my domain sent to a server when I use this checker?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "No. All queries run in your browser against the public resolvers above. PromptDNS has no server in the lookup path and stores nothing." } }
  ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DNS Propagation Checker, Global DNS Test"
  description="Free DNS propagation checker: instantly check whether your DNS records have propagated across public resolvers. Supports A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, TXT, NS, SOA and more."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/tools/" class="hover:text-accent">Tools</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DNS Propagation Checker</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DNS Propagation Checker: Compare Answers Across Global Resolvers</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Free DNS propagation checker: query A, AAAA, MX, TXT, CNAME across Cloudflare, Google and AliDNS resolvers.</p>

    <div class="mt-8">
      <PropagationChecker />
    </div>

    <article class="rich-text mt-14 max-w-none">
      <h2>How this DNS checker works</h2>
      <p>
        The checker fires parallel DNS-over-HTTPS queries and compares the answer sets side by side.
        What it is really measuring is the <strong>cache hierarchy</strong>: your visitors' ISP resolvers,
        the public resolvers, and the authoritative nameserver each hold a copy of your record for
        exactly its TTL. Mismatched answers are not an error, they are a live map of which caches have
        refreshed and which still serve the old value from before the SOA serial was incremented.
      </p>
      <p>
        How long that map keeps changing depends on the record type. Typical windows after a TTL-aware
        change:
      </p>
      <div class="not-prose my-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Record type</th><th>Typical window</th><th>Why</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">A / AAAA</td><td>Minutes to a few hours</td><td>Short TTLs are common; answers are direct IPs</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">CNAME</td><td>Minutes to hours</td><td>Chained lookup: the alias resolves through its target</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">MX / TXT</td><td>Up to the record TTL</td><td>Mail exchangers and verifiers cache aggressively</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">NS</td><td>Up to 48 hours</td><td>Delegation changes touch the parent zone, which caches longest</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">DS / DNSKEY</td><td>Up to 24 to 48 hours</td><td>Validators hold DNSSEC delegation data through the full TTL</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>What "propagated" means here</h2>
      <p>
        A change is fully propagated when every resolver returns the same answer set. If some
        resolvers show old values, the mismatch is expected: they cached your previous record and
        will refresh when its TTL (time-to-live) expires. You can lower your TTL before a planned
        change to make this window shorter.
      </p>

      <h2>Tips for faster propagation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Lower your TTL first</strong> (e.g. to 300 seconds) at least 24 hours before a migration, then make the change.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the old server live</strong> during propagation so cached traffic still works.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the right record type</strong>, a site pointing problem is usually A/AAAA/CNAME; email problems are usually MX/SPF/TXT.</li>
        <li><strong>Flush your local cache</strong> after changes, see <a href="/flush-dns-cache/">how to flush DNS cache</a> on Windows, macOS, Linux, and Chrome.</li>
      </ul>

      
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long does DNS propagation take?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">It is governed by the record's TTL: each resolver may serve the old value until its cached copy expires, so worst case equals the full TTL. New names with no cached history resolve immediately everywhere.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why do some resolvers still show old values?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Their cached copy has not expired yet. The TTL column in the results shows how much longer each resolver may keep serving the old answer.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I speed up propagation?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Not for a change already made. For future changes, lower the TTL to 300 seconds a day before, make the change, then raise it back.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Propagation finished but I still see the old site. Why?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The remaining cache is usually local: your browser, operating system, or ISP resolver keeps its own copy. Flush the local DNS cache and restart the browser.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Which resolvers does this checker query?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Cloudflare (1.1.1.1), Google (8.8.8.8), and AliDNS (223.5.5.5) over DNS-over-HTTPS, directly from your browser. These are three vantage points, not every network on earth.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is my domain sent to a server when I use this checker?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No. All queries run in your browser against the public resolvers above. PromptDNS has no server in the lookup path and stores nothing.</p></details></div>
<h2>Related tools and guides</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/what-is-dns/">What is DNS?</a>, the complete guide</li>
        <li><a href="/dns-propagation/">DNS propagation explained</a>, why it takes time</li>
        <li><a href="/flush-dns-cache/">Flush your DNS cache</a> (all operating systems)</li>
        <li><a href="/mx-record/">MX records explained</a>, email routing</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
